#warning-env(title: [Occult Malignancy])[
Cancer-related fatigue can mimic ME/CFS, particularly in early stages without obvious tumor burden:

    - *Red flags*: Unintentional weight loss, night sweats, fever, lymphadenopathy, age >50 with new-onset fatigue
    - *Screening*: Age-appropriate cancer screening (colonoscopy, mammography); if red flags present, consider CT chest/abdomen/pelvis
    - *Laboratory clues*: Anemia, elevated ESR, abnormal WBC count

The evidence supports *targeted* red-flag investigation rather than blanket screening in
every patient with fatigue: in primary care, malignancy is found in only ~0.6% of tiredness
presentations, so investigation is warranted on specific findings, not routinely @Stadje2016.
But the reverse danger is real — serious organic disease (including malignancy) can be missed
when chronic multi-system symptoms are attributed to a syndromic diagnosis such as ME/CFS
@Kobayashi2022. In a patient already labelled with ME/CFS, a new weight loss, fever, or
localised pain therefore warrants a working diagnosis, not reassurance.

] <warn:malignancy-screening>
